235
00:17:12,821 --> 00:17:21,500
Biometrics is a means of identifying an individual based on a physical attribute or biological

236
00:17:21,501 --> 00:17:23,440
attribute.

237
00:17:23,441 --> 00:17:28,340
The idea of systematic screening by identifying unique body features like scars or tattoos

238
00:17:28,341 --> 00:17:31,260
has been around for millennia.

239
00:17:31,261 --> 00:17:41,000
4,000 years ago, Babylonians used fingerprints on tablets to close business deals.

240
00:17:41,001 --> 00:17:47,000
The attacks of 9-11 created a greater need for biometric securities.

241
00:17:47,001 --> 00:17:52,000
This led to the establishment of Biometric Services International in Morgantown, West Virginia,

242
00:17:52,001 --> 00:17:56,000
a subsidiary of the National Biometric Security Project.

243
00:17:56,001 --> 00:18:03,000
Biometrics is the highest form of security technology that you can currently provide.

244
00:18:03,001 --> 00:18:08,000
One form of biometric security gaining popularity is facial recognition.

245
00:18:08,001 --> 00:18:12,960
While already a popular way to find and identify criminals, it's being adapted for door locks.

246
00:18:13,680 --> 00:18:16,180
This is a 3D facial recognition device.

247
00:18:16,960 --> 00:18:19,160
This right here is the enrollment station.

248
00:18:19,161 --> 00:18:23,920
You would see something much like this on the door if you were coming in to verify,

249
00:18:24,480 --> 00:18:27,720
but this would be your initial stop in the process.

250
00:18:29,160 --> 00:18:33,480
Enrollment allows each device to recognize the face of the user to authorize access.

251
00:18:33,481 --> 00:18:38,240
On the facial recognition device, enrollment means merely getting your picture taken.

252
00:18:38,960 --> 00:18:44,000
Obviously it focuses on the eyes, on the brow, nose, mouth, and chin.

253
00:18:44,260 --> 00:18:48,040
And what it will do, it will take measurements in between these points.

254
00:18:49,260 --> 00:18:53,280
The computer selects different points on your face and develops a map between them,

255
00:18:53,860 --> 00:18:56,340
converting them into a numerical string or code.

256
00:18:57,120 --> 00:19:01,280
This creates a template that the computer will use to identify the person trying to pass.

257
00:19:01,980 --> 00:19:05,660
So from then on, when you present your face to this device,

258
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:08,320
you stare at yourself for a split second and you're in.

259
00:19:09,780 --> 00:19:13,360
While this device uses several points on the face to identify the user,

260
00:19:13,960 --> 00:19:15,480
this one uses only the eye.

261
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:19,100
Over here, we have an iris recognition system.

262
00:19:19,101 --> 00:19:21,560
It basically takes a picture of your iris.

263
00:19:21,840 --> 00:19:26,200
It will make, again, a numerical representation of your iris and then store that.

264
00:19:27,580 --> 00:19:30,580
Much like a fingerprint, each iris is unique.

265
00:19:30,581 --> 00:19:37,320
It has splits, swirls, and different breakpoints that the computer measures and maps, similar to the facial recognition program.

266
00:19:38,020 --> 00:19:42,200
All the user must do from then on is stand in the right spot, and access is granted.

267
00:19:43,160 --> 00:19:44,580
Identification is completed.

268
00:19:46,320 --> 00:19:49,100
But don't try to fool the system with a stolen eyeball.

269
00:19:49,900 --> 00:19:52,800
This does have a liveness detection system in it.

270
00:19:52,880 --> 00:19:56,160
It will check to make sure that the iris in front of it is actually alive.

271
00:19:56,161 --> 00:20:01,500
You can't pull someone's eyeball out like you see in the movies and stick it up to this sort of device.

272
00:20:01,860 --> 00:20:04,260
The iris disintegrates very, very quickly after death.

273
00:20:08,240 --> 00:20:12,300
One of the most widely recognized biometrics is fingerprint recognition.

274
00:20:13,840 --> 00:20:16,760
Identifying fingerprints dates back to the late 19th century.

275
00:20:18,280 --> 00:20:22,359
By the 1920s, fingerprints were used by law enforcement, the U.S.

276
00:20:22,360 --> 00:20:25,560
military, and the FBI as a means of identification.

277
00:20:26,160 --> 00:20:31,160
Using a fingerprint is a way to determine who gets in and who's kept out.

278
00:20:31,161 --> 00:20:34,160
Works the same way as the iris recognition device.

279
00:20:34,161 --> 00:20:39,160
This will look at the ridge patterns on your fingerprints, where this one ends,

280
00:20:39,161 --> 00:20:42,160
where this one ends, where these two split right here.

281
00:20:42,161 --> 00:20:46,160
That could be a place for a marking point.

282
00:20:46,161 --> 00:20:52,160
Want your system super secure? Try a three-factor fingerprint identification device.

283
00:20:52,161 --> 00:20:57,380
How the system works, you'll come up, you'll insert your card in here, you'll enter your PIN number here.

284
00:20:57,760 --> 00:21:03,140
The PIN number will be the PIN number that matches on the card, and it will prompt you to put your fingerprint on the sensor down here.

285
00:21:03,680 --> 00:21:06,340
And then if all those things match up, it will go ahead and grant you access.

286
00:21:07,840 --> 00:21:13,040
This system uses a smart card that features a memory chip containing a biometric template of the user.

287
00:21:13,980 --> 00:21:18,160
The fingerprint must match the template. Only then will the user gain access.

288
00:21:18,161 --> 00:21:23,260
By the way, don't get any ideas about using someone else's finger.

289
00:21:24,000 --> 00:21:25,280
Well, it's not on their hand.

290
00:21:26,460 --> 00:21:30,020
Much like the iris scanner, these systems also feature liveness detection.

291
00:21:31,320 --> 00:21:34,880
All these devices rely on physical patterns that can be seen on the body.

292
00:21:35,320 --> 00:21:38,300
But what about a device that sees patterns on the inside of the body?

293
00:21:38,700 --> 00:21:41,560
This is a vein recognition system that we have in front of us here.

294
00:21:41,860 --> 00:21:44,520
What this technology does is it does go through the skin.

295
00:21:44,521 --> 00:21:49,340
It does use an infrared light to read the actual veins going through your hand,

296
00:21:49,580 --> 00:21:51,720
and it will use those as its pattern.

297
00:21:52,600 --> 00:21:54,840
Like the facial and iris recognition devices,

298
00:21:55,400 --> 00:21:59,200
this uses the pattern to create a numerical code to grant access to the user.

299
00:22:00,000 --> 00:22:01,880
It is sort of like a hidden key.

300
00:22:04,100 --> 00:22:07,200
Biometric locks guarantee that the right people are getting in

301
00:22:07,201 --> 00:22:09,500
and the wrong people are being kept out.

302
00:22:10,640 --> 00:22:13,880
But when you're trying to secure a 2,000-mile border,

303
00:22:14,520 --> 00:22:16,080
you'll need another approach.

304
00:22:18,100 --> 00:22:22,080
In most cases, keeping someone out is an achievable goal.

305
00:22:22,940 --> 00:22:28,080
Whether the restricted area is a safe, a nuclear waste site, or a cutting-edge lab.

306
00:22:28,800 --> 00:22:30,560
But what if the area is a whole country?

307
00:22:31,380 --> 00:22:32,460
Things get a lot tougher.

308
00:22:34,080 --> 00:22:38,200
Since 9-11, most governments around the world have beefed up keep-out policies

309
00:22:38,201 --> 00:22:40,420
and enforced them with border control.

310
00:22:40,421 --> 00:22:44,381
At a yearly cost of billions of dollars, the U.S.

311
00:22:44,382 --> 00:22:51,171
Customs and Border Protection oversees nearly 2,000 miles of border between the U.S.

312
00:22:51,172 --> 00:22:52,060
and Mexico.

313
00:22:53,980 --> 00:22:58,700
That distance makes it the world's longest heavily patrolled border between neighboring countries.

314
00:23:00,520 --> 00:23:03,160
Certain spots see much more activity than others.

315
00:23:04,160 --> 00:23:10,700
The San Ysidro Port of Entry, just south of San Diego, California, manages a massive amount of border traffic.

316
00:23:15,660 --> 00:23:18,440
We're here at the San Ysidro Port of Entry, busiest land board in the world.

317
00:23:18,960 --> 00:23:22,640
We see over 40,000 to 50,000 cars a day, 25,000 pedestrians.

318
00:23:22,780 --> 00:23:26,520
We add everybody up. It's just about 100,000 people a day through this one port.

319
00:23:28,400 --> 00:23:32,940
That's about 36 million people passing through this single border point each year.

320
00:23:33,160 --> 00:23:37,220
But the officers here are doing more than looking for unlawful entrance.

321
00:23:41,280 --> 00:23:45,020
They're on the hunt for terrorists, drugs, and banned agriculture.

322
00:23:46,500 --> 00:23:50,900
On an average day, we go between 100 and 200 people that we catch with violations of the law.

323
00:23:51,740 --> 00:23:57,060
These long lines might be a nuisance, but having them allows extra time to find violators.

324
00:23:58,080 --> 00:24:00,960
We have teams of officers who work with and without dogs,

325
00:24:00,961 --> 00:24:03,420
and they're capable of detecting a lot of things before you get to the booth.

326
00:24:04,580 --> 00:24:09,880
The dogs' keen sense of smell is key in sniffing out weapons and drugs, money and people.

327
00:24:12,540 --> 00:24:16,200
The dogs are just one of the filters Customs and Border Protection uses

328
00:24:16,201 --> 00:24:18,720
to screen intruders before they even reach an officer.

329
00:24:20,920 --> 00:24:23,760
But their system hasn't always been this efficient and secure.

330
00:24:24,780 --> 00:24:29,120
A lot has changed, especially since 9-11, like these poles, for example, we have out here,

331
00:24:29,121 --> 00:24:31,400
and that's to prevent people from going in between lanes,

332
00:24:31,640 --> 00:24:32,460
what we call port runners,

333
00:24:32,600 --> 00:24:34,380
trying to escape from the inspection process.

334
00:24:35,480 --> 00:24:37,580
This is our radio frequency ID reader,

335
00:24:37,780 --> 00:24:40,400
and what this does for us, people can drive past

336
00:24:40,401 --> 00:24:41,460
and we'll have their information

337
00:24:41,461 --> 00:24:43,480
before they even get to the officer at the primary booth.

338
00:24:45,720 --> 00:24:47,560
This is our license plate reader technology,

339
00:24:47,780 --> 00:24:49,940
so if there's a stolen car or anything else about the plate,

340
00:24:50,020 --> 00:24:52,460
we will know that before the person gets to the inspection booth.

341
00:24:55,480 --> 00:24:57,420
Those who pass the battery of inspections

342
00:24:57,421 --> 00:24:58,800
and document verification

343
00:24:58,801 --> 00:25:01,060
are allowed to enter the United States.

344
00:25:01,860 --> 00:25:03,480
Others go to a secondary inspection,

345
00:25:03,820 --> 00:25:06,720
where vehicles are subjected to a more detailed search.

346
00:25:07,260 --> 00:25:08,960
Sometimes they're even driven through an X-ray.

347
00:25:10,940 --> 00:25:14,700
Not all illegal crossings happen at the 39 land ports of entry.

348
00:25:15,640 --> 00:25:18,280
In fact, most violators try to avoid the ports altogether.

349
00:25:19,820 --> 00:25:21,700
That's where Border Patrol steps in.

350
00:25:22,240 --> 00:25:27,040
And in San Ysidro, it all starts with two distinct fences over five miles in length.

351
00:25:27,041 --> 00:25:32,040
Anyone that's in this area, we know they're either with us or they're here illegally.

352
00:25:32,041 --> 00:25:36,040
Prior to 1994, this area was overrun by alien traffic.

353
00:25:36,041 --> 00:25:39,040
We had groups in the hundreds that would make bonsai runs,

354
00:25:39,041 --> 00:25:44,040
and we didn't have enough manpower, technology, infrastructure, anything,

355
00:25:44,041 --> 00:25:47,040
to withstand that kind of alien traffic.

356
00:25:48,040 --> 00:25:53,040
As of 2008, 500 miles of fencing from California to Texas

357
00:25:53,041 --> 00:25:56,040
stands between the United States and Mexico.

358
00:25:56,041 --> 00:25:59,040
More fencing is planned.

359
00:25:59,041 --> 00:26:04,040
Even though extra fencing and frequent patrols have slowed illegal traffic near the San Ysidro border,

360
00:26:04,041 --> 00:26:07,040
the area is not without its dangers.

361
00:26:07,041 --> 00:26:10,040
We've just taken out over 2,000 feet of slats here.

362
00:26:10,041 --> 00:26:14,040
The reason why we did that is because we had so many assaults in this area.

363
00:26:14,041 --> 00:26:20,040
We wanted to know where these individuals were standing when they were throwing these large rocks and bottles at our agents.

364
00:26:20,041 --> 00:26:24,040
A quarter of all assaults in the United States happened in San Diego,

365
00:26:24,041 --> 00:26:27,380
and close to 80% of those assaults happened in the area that we're in.

366
00:26:27,680 --> 00:26:30,560
To contend with individuals jumping the primary fence,

367
00:26:30,940 --> 00:26:35,120
the Army Corps of Engineers constructed a secondary fence for the Border Patrol Agency.

368
00:26:36,120 --> 00:26:37,880
Behind me is a secondary fence.

369
00:26:38,180 --> 00:26:41,940
It was put up in 1996 to help us control this area.

370
00:26:42,480 --> 00:26:46,840
On top of it, we put on concertina wire towards the end of 2007.

371
00:26:47,740 --> 00:26:49,620
The road here may be smooth,

372
00:26:50,060 --> 00:26:52,980
but most of the border areas involve rubber terrain.

373
00:26:53,900 --> 00:26:57,780
Officers must use ATVs and horses to patrol and secure those areas.

374
00:26:59,600 --> 00:27:02,920
Still, the Border Patrol uses more than just eyes on the ground.

375
00:27:05,340 --> 00:27:08,380
This is a remote video surveillance system control center.

376
00:27:09,160 --> 00:27:11,340
It's one of many centers stationed along the border.

377
00:27:12,200 --> 00:27:15,980
The activity that gets observed here gets relayed to the agents in the field

378
00:27:15,981 --> 00:27:21,400
so that they can go and respond to it and take care of whatever's happening out in the area.

379
00:27:21,401 --> 00:27:26,160
The camera system monitors a five-mile stretch between two ports of entry.

380
00:27:27,160 --> 00:27:30,740
Each operator views four camera feeds, depending on the level of activity.

381
00:27:32,700 --> 00:27:35,520
But the darkness of night provides additional challenges.

382
00:27:38,420 --> 00:27:42,880
Positioned on high hilltops are night vision scopes equipped with infrared technology.

383
00:27:43,740 --> 00:27:46,020
Each scope is monitored by an agent from his vehicle.

384
00:27:46,900 --> 00:27:49,700
The scope can see about a mile out for precise people

385
00:27:49,701 --> 00:27:52,340
and still be able to count how many people are running

386
00:27:52,341 --> 00:27:54,040
or how many people are crossing the border.

387
00:27:54,280 --> 00:27:58,280
It's a multiple controller just like this, up, down, left, right.

388
00:27:58,600 --> 00:28:02,920
And also you can zoom in on the individuals and the area if you think it's suspect.

389
00:28:03,960 --> 00:28:06,220
This isn't just a night vision video game.

390
00:28:06,680 --> 00:28:07,680
The stakes are high.

391
00:28:08,480 --> 00:28:11,520
You've got to know your area really well to be able to relay to the people

392
00:28:11,521 --> 00:28:14,320
who are down there on the field and tell them exactly how to come in.

393
00:28:14,380 --> 00:28:15,960
They can't see anything. It's dark for them.

394
00:28:16,020 --> 00:28:19,120
all they know is that there's someone out there and it may be close to them.

395
00:28:19,940 --> 00:28:22,100
In spite of all these sophisticated measures,

396
00:28:23,020 --> 00:28:27,735
some organizations estimate that over 500,000 illegally cross the U.S.

397
00:28:27,736 --> 00:28:28,880
border each year.

398
00:28:33,160 --> 00:28:36,880
While Customs and Border Protection utilizes perimeters and barriers,

399
00:28:38,200 --> 00:28:39,660
many others use them as well.

400
00:28:40,460 --> 00:28:45,020
Places of high security like banks, military facilities and chemical plants

401
00:28:45,021 --> 00:28:48,380
all depend on barriers as a means of keeping out intruders.

402
00:28:49,160 --> 00:28:52,020
Most are permanent barriers in the form of large blocks,

403
00:28:52,560 --> 00:28:53,920
while others are movable,

404
00:28:54,740 --> 00:28:56,500
allowing access to approved visitors.

405
00:28:57,460 --> 00:29:00,760
One thing they all have in common is they have to be tested.

406
00:29:02,380 --> 00:29:05,860
Companies in the barrier business turn to the Federal Highway Administration

407
00:29:05,861 --> 00:29:09,400
and U.S. Department of State to test and approve their products.

408
00:29:10,200 --> 00:29:12,440
In crash, after crash,

409
00:29:13,280 --> 00:29:14,560
after crash,

410
00:29:15,020 --> 00:29:25,660
The current standard rating companies test for is the M50, meaning a barrier will stop a 15,000-pound vehicle traveling at 50 miles per hour.

411
00:29:26,080 --> 00:29:28,580
But there's more than one way to stop a moving truck.

412
00:29:29,980 --> 00:29:38,500
The Ground Retractable Automobile Barrier, or GRAB, is a technology developed by Universal Safety Response, or USR.

413
00:29:39,020 --> 00:29:43,680
It was developed after the creator and president of USR witnessed a fatal car crash.

414
00:29:44,560 --> 00:29:47,040
Shortly after that, I was watching the History Channel,

415
00:29:47,120 --> 00:29:51,760
and I saw the old World War II aircrafts where if the planes missed the arresting wire,

416
00:29:51,900 --> 00:29:53,940
they got stopped by a net system, and I was thinking,

417
00:29:54,100 --> 00:29:56,920
if you can stop a plane, you ought to be able to stop a vehicle.

418
00:29:58,880 --> 00:30:01,480
USR designed the grab to be a non-lethal barrier.

419
00:30:02,220 --> 00:30:04,340
It also wanted to make the grab easy to operate.

420
00:30:05,080 --> 00:30:07,640
The grab system is an active vehicle barrier system,

421
00:30:07,641 --> 00:30:11,720
and what that means is it either allows or denies access to vehicles

422
00:30:11,721 --> 00:30:13,680
who are looking to enter or exit a facility.

423
00:30:15,000 --> 00:30:16,720
With the simple push of a button,

424
00:30:17,180 --> 00:30:20,440
the barrier raises or lowers in two seconds or less.

425
00:30:21,300 --> 00:30:24,380
But could a non-lethal net system work as well as other barriers?

426
00:30:26,180 --> 00:30:28,740
In addition to 14 successful crash tests,

427
00:30:28,860 --> 00:30:32,820
the grab system has also been struck over 115 times in the real world

428
00:30:32,821 --> 00:30:34,860
at systems installed across the country,

429
00:30:35,100 --> 00:30:38,340
and the vehicle occupants escaped without significant injuries,

430
00:30:38,500 --> 00:30:40,240
suffering no more than a seatbelt bruise.

431
00:30:41,720 --> 00:30:47,620
Since opening in 2001, USR has delivered over 300 grab systems.

432
00:30:48,880 --> 00:30:52,500
While Customs and Border Protection stops terrorists on land,

433
00:30:53,760 --> 00:30:57,040
what kinds of technologies keep the unwanted out of our skies?

434
00:31:02,040 --> 00:31:03,840
Locks keep out intruders.

435
00:31:04,680 --> 00:31:06,740
Barriers also keep out the unwanted.

436
00:31:07,420 --> 00:31:09,420
But what if the approach is from the skies?

437
00:31:11,720 --> 00:31:22,300
This is Customs and Border Protection's Air and Marine Operations Center, or AMOC, in Riverside, California.

438
00:31:23,200 --> 00:31:26,679
The job of the staff here is to monitor all U.S.

439
00:31:26,680 --> 00:31:31,100
airspace for general aviation, 24 hours a day, 7 days a week.

440
00:31:31,880 --> 00:31:36,580
Our responsibility is to know who they are and that they go where they're supposed to.

441
00:31:37,020 --> 00:31:41,640
Each detection enforcement officer here is responsible for a specific area.

442
00:31:41,720 --> 00:31:47,820
California, Arizona, New Mexico, Texas, and you can go across the southern border and then northern border.

443
00:31:53,980 --> 00:32:02,440
With more than 87,000 daily flights in the U.S., it's necessary to break up the surveillance into sections, especially during the day.

444
00:32:03,180 --> 00:32:05,000
Generally, that's when most people fly.

445
00:32:05,001 --> 00:32:12,320
So 16,000 aircraft, of which 77% of them are general aviation aircraft coming into the United States.

446
00:32:12,820 --> 00:32:19,500
So when you go out and look at an entire area, it's very difficult to pinpoint one specific aircraft.

447
00:32:20,780 --> 00:32:22,620
Radar is key to this operation.

448
00:32:23,700 --> 00:32:29,840
Combining ground-based and airborne radars, the facility is capable of displaying 450 radars at once.

449
00:32:30,700 --> 00:32:33,180
But this isn't just a big green scope that beeps.

450
00:32:33,181 --> 00:32:38,420
We're sitting in front of what we call the Air Marine Operations Surveillance System, the AMOS.

451
00:32:39,100 --> 00:32:45,640
It's our system that displays not only all of the radars that we have into the system, but also the air tracks.

452
00:32:47,480 --> 00:32:51,920
From this station, operators can select any plane and be able to see its flight pattern,

453
00:32:52,360 --> 00:32:57,120
where it departed, where it's supposed to land, as well as a two-year history of the aircraft.

454
00:32:57,440 --> 00:33:02,640
And if immediate action is needed, the system can even tell the operator where the plane will be in the near future.

455
00:33:03,180 --> 00:33:09,720
If it is of interest to me, then I want to find out where is the track going to be in, let's say, 10 minutes.

456
00:33:09,940 --> 00:33:18,960
I can also ask it that, and we'll display a 10-minute vector that will also allow me to tell whether that aircraft is maneuvering east, west, or whatever direction.

457
00:33:20,860 --> 00:33:24,100
Some sites in the United States get extra attention from the operators.

458
00:33:25,020 --> 00:33:30,140
Like the nation's capital, when it comes to protecting airspace, it's hard not to think of 9-11.

459
00:33:30,141 --> 00:33:34,300
We've always been involved in the terrorist activity even before that.

460
00:33:34,720 --> 00:33:37,780
But 9-11 brought a heightened security alert,

461
00:33:38,260 --> 00:33:43,400
so we're very much centered on not only who's the pilot of that aircraft,

462
00:33:43,500 --> 00:33:46,140
but now we need to know who the passengers of an aircraft are.

463
00:33:47,360 --> 00:33:51,780
Because of 9-11, the airspace around the area known as the nation's capital region

464
00:33:51,781 --> 00:33:53,900
is monitored even closer than before.

465
00:33:54,220 --> 00:33:59,940
Any aircraft that comes into this 30 miles of airspace has to be talking to the FAA.

466
00:34:00,140 --> 00:34:04,860
If there isn't another 9-11 type of event coming into the NCR airspace,

467
00:34:05,280 --> 00:34:08,080
it is pointed out over that domestic events network.

468
00:34:08,280 --> 00:34:09,920
It is a telephone-based system.

469
00:34:12,140 --> 00:34:16,000
That line is used not only in the national capital region,

470
00:34:16,220 --> 00:34:18,920
but the entire United States as well.

471
00:34:18,921 --> 00:34:21,380
When the VIPs move, the president moves.

472
00:34:23,500 --> 00:34:27,680
Maps can be layered onto the radar system to see exactly where a plane might be,

473
00:34:27,681 --> 00:34:30,680
as well as the terrain of the area that it's flying over.

474
00:34:31,720 --> 00:34:34,060
Additionally, the AMOC has top-secret plans

475
00:34:34,061 --> 00:34:36,360
if something like 9-11 would ever happen again.

476
00:34:38,820 --> 00:34:41,020
As part of the Office of Air and Marine,

477
00:34:41,660 --> 00:34:45,180
AMOC can launch law enforcement aircraft to monitor targets of interest.

478
00:34:46,400 --> 00:34:49,980
Air and Marine craft include Black Hawk helicopters and Citation jets,

479
00:34:49,981 --> 00:34:52,860
the fastest craft in the Air and Marine fleet.

480
00:34:52,861 --> 00:34:59,700
But perhaps one of the most advantageous craft AMOC has at its disposal

481
00:34:59,701 --> 00:35:02,980
doesn't require a pilot at all, at least not in the air.

482
00:35:03,540 --> 00:35:06,440
Where we are right now is in the ground control station.

483
00:35:06,441 --> 00:35:09,840
It's the unmanned aircraft system control station

484
00:35:09,841 --> 00:35:13,600
that we use to fly the unmanned aircraft.

485
00:35:14,100 --> 00:35:18,520
This is the Predator B, and it can fly upwards of 50,000 feet

486
00:35:18,521 --> 00:35:21,760
and can be in flight for over 12 hours at a time.

487
00:35:21,761 --> 00:35:26,860
It's equipped with an infrared sensor and the ability to send a live feed to the control station.

488
00:35:27,460 --> 00:35:29,360
We control everything from right here.

489
00:35:29,500 --> 00:35:34,560
We have a pilot that sits in the left seat, a sensor operator that is in the right seat.

490
00:35:35,180 --> 00:35:40,040
The sensor operator is responsible for working the camera, working the IRs,

491
00:35:40,200 --> 00:35:45,660
slewing it to the positions that we need to go, and the pilot's flying the aircraft.

492
00:35:46,140 --> 00:35:49,680
A technician is also present to keep an eye out for any mechanical problems.

493
00:35:51,760 --> 00:35:56,020
The agency continues to build its unmanned fleet to help secure the nation's airspace.

494
00:35:56,940 --> 00:35:58,880
But the most important question remains.

495
00:35:59,600 --> 00:36:02,600
What happens when AMOC finds a potential danger in the sky?

496
00:36:03,540 --> 00:36:07,440
We are there to meet that aircraft and the security role,

497
00:36:07,441 --> 00:36:12,480
we will track him and take that aircraft to ground and interview that pilot appropriately.

498
00:36:12,720 --> 00:36:13,420
What's your name, sir?

499
00:36:13,920 --> 00:36:18,620
But if the pilot doesn't cooperate while in flight, or becomes more of a threat,

500
00:36:21,760 --> 00:36:23,700
DOD muscle takes over.

501
00:36:24,420 --> 00:36:26,680
We don't have any aircraft that are armed,

502
00:36:26,960 --> 00:36:28,900
so if it comes to a terrorist activity,

503
00:36:29,080 --> 00:36:31,580
they will take care of the shooting down part of it.

504
00:36:33,500 --> 00:36:36,980
AMOC is constantly vigilant to keep winged dangers out of the skies.

505
00:36:37,840 --> 00:36:40,920
But how do researchers keep out a sharp-toothed threat

506
00:36:40,921 --> 00:36:42,200
that lurks in the waters?

507
00:36:48,620 --> 00:36:51,180
This site is one that fills many with fear.

508
00:36:51,760 --> 00:36:55,760
Shark attacks on humans occur nearly 70 times a year.

509
00:36:55,761 --> 00:36:59,760
Of course, the safest way to avoid sharks would be to stay out of the water.

510
00:37:01,760 --> 00:37:04,760
However, these researchers don't have a choice.

511
00:37:04,761 --> 00:37:08,760
In order to study sharks, they must enter their environment.

512
00:37:08,761 --> 00:37:11,760
The safest way to do that is in one of these.

513
00:37:15,760 --> 00:37:20,760
Marine biologist Luke Tippel has been diving with sharks professionally since 2000

514
00:37:20,761 --> 00:37:23,700
and knows the true keep-out value of a cage.

515
00:37:25,080 --> 00:37:27,660
A shark cage actually lets us get close to the animals

516
00:37:27,661 --> 00:37:30,400
and observe their behavior without worrying about

517
00:37:30,401 --> 00:37:32,560
how their behavior might affect us adversely.

518
00:37:33,320 --> 00:37:36,140
The cage is designed so that our divers can stay inside

519
00:37:36,141 --> 00:37:37,400
and the sharks can stay outside,

520
00:37:37,660 --> 00:37:39,560
but it's completely useless if we can't see them.

521
00:37:39,900 --> 00:37:42,160
So we built in the aperture at a height

522
00:37:42,161 --> 00:37:44,460
where the sharks usually stay around this level,

523
00:37:44,640 --> 00:37:47,260
but if they do come down, the window is small enough,

524
00:37:47,560 --> 00:37:49,380
they'd bang into it and usually turn away.

525
00:37:49,381 --> 00:37:52,720
it's very rare that a shark might get stuck or go through one of these windows.

526
00:37:53,560 --> 00:37:56,700
In fact, the cage itself is a visual deterrent to sharks.

527
00:37:57,440 --> 00:38:00,620
The sharks actually have very good eyesight, so they can see the cage.

528
00:38:00,700 --> 00:38:03,140
It's not something that they want to charge into and bang into.

529
00:38:03,560 --> 00:38:06,740
They can certainly also sense it. It gives off an electrical signal in the water.

530
00:38:07,260 --> 00:38:12,620
So the sharks will be curious about it, but it's not something they actually want to bite or feel endangered by.

531
00:38:13,060 --> 00:38:16,240
It'd take a lot for a shark to actually bite onto a cage,

532
00:38:16,640 --> 00:38:22,660
But if it did accidentally or intentionally bite onto the cage, a shark's bite actually isn't all that strong.

533
00:38:23,000 --> 00:38:26,960
It's yet to be measured very well, but it's really not strong enough to bite through the aluminum.

534
00:38:27,400 --> 00:38:31,340
It would probably just bite it, it would feel something wasn't right about it, and let it go.

535
00:38:32,060 --> 00:38:33,940
Sharks don't usually bite cages.

536
00:38:35,040 --> 00:38:41,420
Misuse of bait, like dead fish and blood, called chum by amateur divers, is often responsible for such incidents.

537
00:38:41,421 --> 00:38:45,760
But even if cages don't look tasty to sharks,

538
00:38:46,040 --> 00:38:48,820
they must still be specially designed and constructed.

539
00:38:52,380 --> 00:38:53,860
This is marine-grade aluminum,

540
00:38:54,360 --> 00:38:58,360
and we'll be using, like, a marine-grade aluminum TIG rod, too.

541
00:38:59,200 --> 00:39:02,160
With the shark cages, usually everything we do is TIG welded.

542
00:39:02,460 --> 00:39:05,380
We get a better penetration, stronger weld,

543
00:39:05,920 --> 00:39:07,760
a lot better for high strength

544
00:39:07,761 --> 00:39:09,940
to try and keep the sharks out of the cage, pretty much,

545
00:39:09,941 --> 00:39:10,740
and keep them from breaking.

546
00:39:11,420 --> 00:39:16,020
And each cage is built for the sharks that inhabit the area in which it will be used.

547
00:39:17,380 --> 00:39:22,620
We usually run like a 12-inch spacing from center to center to try and keep the sharks out.

548
00:39:23,160 --> 00:39:26,320
If they're dealing with smaller sharks, we've done smaller spacing on our centers.

549
00:39:26,800 --> 00:39:34,000
This cage is going in great white territory, so realistically, 12 inches on center is more than adequate for the size of the sharks.

550
00:39:35,300 --> 00:39:39,020
Aluminum is a good choice for the cages because it won't corrode quickly in the water.

551
00:39:40,220 --> 00:39:41,160
Steel and iron will.

552
00:39:41,420 --> 00:39:45,420
When complete, this cage will weigh about 400 pounds.

553
00:39:45,421 --> 00:39:49,420
Researchers can also use the viewing windows for gathering samples.

554
00:39:49,421 --> 00:39:52,420
Just be careful where you stick your arms.

555
00:39:52,421 --> 00:39:57,420
The most efficient way to get any type of sample from the sharks when researchers are underwater

556
00:39:57,421 --> 00:40:00,420
is actually to just go straight through these bars.

557
00:40:00,421 --> 00:40:03,420
We can reach out and use a spear gun or a spear tip,

558
00:40:03,421 --> 00:40:07,420
and we can take small biopsies from the sharks. We can also tag them.

559
00:40:07,421 --> 00:40:11,580
But what if you don't have the luxury of a cage?

560
00:40:11,581 --> 00:40:15,180
There are some other deterrents out there to keep swimmers and divers safe.

561
00:40:15,181 --> 00:40:18,100
Your everyday swimmer really doesn't need to worry about sharks.

562
00:40:18,101 --> 00:40:21,260
Yes, they're in the water, but you're really not on the menu.

563
00:40:21,261 --> 00:40:24,840
If you choose to be extra cautious, you could perhaps wear a shark shield or some other

564
00:40:24,841 --> 00:40:29,300
device which actively repels the sharks that might come into the general area that you're

565
00:40:29,301 --> 00:40:31,420
swimming in.

566
00:40:31,421 --> 00:40:35,840
The shark shield emits an electrical field, essentially creating a protective bubble around

567
00:40:35,841 --> 00:40:37,100
the swimmer.

568
00:40:37,101 --> 00:40:39,480
The range is over 20 feet.

569
00:40:39,481 --> 00:40:43,500
Once a shark is close enough to sense that bubble, it will keep out.

570
00:40:43,501 --> 00:40:46,400
But not all deterrents have the desired result.

571
00:40:46,401 --> 00:40:50,380
The effectiveness of some of the shark repellents on the market is a little bit dubious.

572
00:40:50,381 --> 00:40:54,680
Some, like a shark repelling magnet perhaps, might work in test scenarios, but in the open

573
00:40:54,681 --> 00:40:58,720
ocean, just worn on the wrist, it's probably not going to repel a big shark.

574
00:40:58,721 --> 00:41:03,160
In fact, last year I fed one of these to a great white shark.

575
00:41:03,161 --> 00:41:06,780
There's also the low-tech way to keep a shark out of your space.

576
00:41:06,781 --> 00:41:09,640
Sometimes the least technical piece of equipment is often the most effective.

577
00:41:10,140 --> 00:41:13,540
This pokey stick is actually a very trusted implement for me.

578
00:41:13,940 --> 00:41:17,820
Whether we're tagging or taking photos, they can come just a little too close for comfort.

579
00:41:18,300 --> 00:41:22,320
Actually, frequently I've had to use a stick just to tap them on the nose to keep them at bay.

580
00:41:23,720 --> 00:41:26,620
So the shark may not be able to read signs like us.

581
00:41:26,621 --> 00:41:32,620
But whether it's a blunt stick, an electrical field, or a metal cage, he's still...

